        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "47bffa67-6604-5cfa-924e-9d48bf32b572",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Lester James Peries, Jean Vigo, Tissa Abeysekara, Ravindra Randeniya, Malini Fonseka\n- Works: Rekava, Gamperaliya, Nidhanaya, Golu Hadawatha, Kaliyugaya, Awaragira, Yuganthaya, Wekande Walauwa, Delovak Athara\n- Roles: film director, screenwriter, film producer\n- Concepts: Sinhala Cinema, poetic realism, French New Wave\n- Places: Sri Lanka, France\n\nFacts:\n- Jean Vigo was a French film director born on 26 April 1905 and died on 5 October 1934.\n- In the 1930s, Jean Vigo helped establish poetic realism in film.\n- Jean Vigo became a posthumous influence on the French New Wave of the late 1950s and early 1960s.\n- Lester James Peries was born on 5 April 1919 and is a Sri Lankan film director, screenwriter, and film producer.\n- Lester James Peries had been an active filmmaker since 1949 and was involved in more than 28 films, including shorts and documentaries.\n- Delovak Athara is a 1966 Sri Lankan drama film directed by Lester James Peries, with screenplay, dialogue, and script by Tissa Abeysekara.\n- Lester James Peries received critical acclaim for directing Rekava, Gamperaliya, Nidhanaya, Golu Hadawatha, Kaliyugaya, Awaragira, and Yuganthaya.\n- Wekande Walauwa, directed by Lester James Peries and starring Ravindra Randeniya and Malini Fonseka, was Sri Lanka's first submission for the Academy Awards.\n- Lester James Peries's films often depict Sri Lankan family life in rural settings and conflicted characters.\n- Lester James Peries helped create an authentic expression of Sinhala Cinema.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
